// Tutorial 51: Your first window
//
// Run it:      cabby 051-first-window.cby
// Library:     use gui
// Topics:      gui.window, labels, buttons, text boxes, handlers
//
// A window is an object made from gui.window. You describe what is in it
// in a method called `build`, and say what should happen when things are
// clicked or typed. Then `run` shows it:
//
//     new window -> build: add things, attach handlers -> run
//
// While the window is open the program sleeps, using no processor time,
// until something happens. Then it calls your handler, the way an
// interrupt calls its service routine. `run` returns when the window is
// closed.
//
// What goes where (the manual's "The build method" has the whole story):
//
//   build              runs once, just before the window appears: the
//                      title and size, the widgets, attaching handlers,
//                      starting timers, and starting values for fields.
//                      Keep it quick, and don't wait in it for the user.
//   the object's body  only fields (starting at zero) and methods. A line
//                      that does something, like `add_label "Hi"` or
//                      `every time.seconds(1), tick`, goes inside a method.
//   the top level      `use`, constants, plain functions, and main, which
//                      makes the window and calls run.

use gui

object greeter : gui.window {
	// The things the handlers need later are kept as fields.
	gui.label Message
	gui.entry Name

	void build {
		set_title "Greeter"
		set_size 360, 180

		// Things appear top to bottom in the order they are added.
		Message = add_label "What's your name?"
		Name = add_entry
		gui.button Hello = add_button "Say hello"

		// Handlers: name one of this window's methods. It is not called
		// now; it is called each time the button is clicked.
		Hello.on_click say_hello
		Name.on_enter say_hello          // pressing Enter in the box does the same
	}

	// A handler: a method of the window, with no parameters.
	void say_hello {
		str Who = Name.text              // what is typed in the box
		if Who.length == 0 {
			Message.set_text "Type your name first!"
			return
		}
		Message.set_text "Hello, " + Who + "!"
		println "greeted", Who
	}
}

int main {
	greeter g
	g.run                                // returns when the window is closed
	println "goodbye"
}

// (For Cabby's test suite: what to do in the window, as a person would.
// Without a test script, the window simply waits for you.)
// script: click Say hello; type 1 Ada; click Say hello; type 1 Grace; enter 1; close
//
// Try this:
//  - Add a second button, "Clear", whose handler empties the box with
//    `Name.set_text ""`.
//  - Make a handler count how many people have been greeted, and show the
//    count in another label with `count.text`.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- output
//> greeted Ada
//> greeted Grace
//> goodbye
